# Backend

This is the Go API for Inspirate Consulting. It receives HTTP requests from the
frontend, checks authentication, runs application logic, and reads or writes
data in PostgreSQL. The API is served by [Fiber](https://gofiber.io/), routes
and API documentation are defined with [Huma](https://huma.rocks/), and
PostgreSQL is accessed with `pgx`.

Start with the [setup guide](../SETUP.md) for local tools, environment
variables, Supabase, and running the application.

## How the backend is structured

Each layer has one job. Requests move downward; results and errors move back
upward:

```text
Fiber server and middleware
				-> Huma route
				-> feature handler
				-> repository interface
				-> PostgreSQL repository
				-> Supabase PostgreSQL database
```

- [`cmd`](cmd) starts the process, loads configuration, and shuts the server
	down cleanly.
- [`internal/config`](internal/config) describes configuration loaded from
	environment variables.
- [`internal/routes`](internal/routes/README.md) creates Fiber and Huma,
	installs middleware, and registers endpoints.
- [`internal/handlers`](internal/handlers/README.md) contains feature logic.
- [`internal/models`](internal/models/README.md) defines request and response
	shapes used by Huma and OpenAPI.
- [`internal/data`](internal/data/README.md) hides database details behind Go
	interfaces. Its [PostgreSQL guide](internal/data/postgres/README.md) explains
	the concrete database implementation.
- [`internal/auth`](internal/auth/README.md) verifies Supabase JWT cookies.
- [`internal/supabase`](internal/supabase/README.md) contains local Supabase
	configuration and database migrations.

## Why use layers?

Keeping HTTP, feature logic, and SQL separate makes each part easier to change
and test. A handler depends on a repository interface, not on PostgreSQL
syntax. Tests can therefore provide a mock repository without starting a
database. A new feature normally adds one route, one handler, one model, one
repository interface, and one PostgreSQL implementation.

## Adding a feature

Follow the existing greeting feature from the outside inward:
(If setting things up in a different order makes more sense to you, go for it.
This is just a recommended approach if you don't know where to start.)

1. Define request and response models in [`internal/models`](internal/models).
2. Add a repository interface in [`internal/data/data.go`](internal/data/data.go).
3. Implement the SQL in `internal/data/postgres/schema/<feature>` and wire it
	 into `data.NewRepository`.
4. Add handler logic in [`internal/handlers`](internal/handlers).
5. Register the endpoint in [`internal/routes`](internal/routes).
6. Add a Supabase migration if the database schema changes.

Read the linked package guides before changing a layer; each one explains its
responsibility and the next place to look.